Task:
The function below is supposed to find and return the largest number in a list, but it doesn't always work correctly. 
Your job is to find the bug and fix it.

Requirements

Function name: find_max (do not rename it)
Takes one parameter: a non-empty list of numbers, numbers
Returns the single largest number found in the list
The fix should work correctly no matter what kind of numbers are in the list — positive, negative, or a mix
Examples

find_max([3, 7, 2]) → 7
find_max([-5, -1, -9]) → -1 (the largest of three negative numbers)
find_max([4]) → 4 (a list with only one number)
find_max([100, 50, 100]) → 100 (duplicate largest values are fine)
Constraints

The list will always contain at least one number — you don't need to handle an empty list.
The list may contain only negative numbers.
The list may contain only one number.
Do not change the function name or its parameter.
You may rewrite the function body however you like, as long as it correctly returns the largest number.
Note: Your solution will be tested against multiple cases, including a list of only negative numbers and a list with just one element — pay close attention to how the search for the largest value gets started.

